{"slug": "praline-charlotte", "nyt_id": 1085, "url": "https://cooking.nytimes.com/recipes/1085-praline-charlotte", "name": "Praline Charlotte", "author": "Molly O’Neill", "description": "", "servings": {"text": "6 - 8 servings", "min": 6, "max": 8, "yield_type": "servings"}, "time": {"prep_minutes": null, "cook_minutes": null, "total_minutes": 300, "raw": {"total": "1 hour, plus 4 hours' refrigeration"}}, "ingredients": [{"position": 1, "raw": "1 ¼ cups sugar", "group": null, "quantity": 1.25, "quantity_max": null, "unit": "cup", "item": "sugar", "preparation": null, "optional": false}, {"position": 2, "raw": "1 ¼ cups pecan halves", "group": null, "quantity": 1.25, "quantity_max": null, "unit": "cup", "item": "pecan halves", "preparation": null, "optional": false}, {"position": 3, "raw": "1 recipe warm creme anglaise (recipe follows)", "group": null, "quantity": 1.0, "quantity_max": null, "unit": null, "item": "recipe warm creme anglaise (recipe follows)", "preparation": null, "optional": false}, {"position": 4, "raw": "1 tablespoon gelatin", "group": null, "quantity": 1.0, "quantity_max": null, "unit": "tbsp", "item": "gelatin", "preparation": null, "optional": false}, {"position": 5, "raw": "⅛ teaspoon salt", "group": null, "quantity": 0.125, "quantity_max": null, "unit": "tsp", "item": "salt", "preparation": null, "optional": false}, {"position": 6, "raw": "2 ¼ cups heavy cream", "group": null, "quantity": 2.25, "quantity_max": null, "unit": "cup", "item": "heavy cream", "preparation": null, "optional": false}, {"position": 7, "raw": "16 to 21 soft ladyfingers, homemade or store-bought", "group": null, "quantity": 16.0, "quantity_max": 21.0, "unit": null, "item": "soft ladyfingers", "preparation": "homemade or store-bought", "optional": false}, {"position": 8, "raw": "¼ cup powdered sugar", "group": null, "quantity": 0.25, "quantity_max": null, "unit": "cup", "item": "powdered sugar", "preparation": null, "optional": false}, {"position": 9, "raw": "1 tablespoon light rum", "group": null, "quantity": 1.0, "quantity_max": null, "unit": "tbsp", "item": "light rum", "preparation": null, "optional": false}], "steps": [{"position": 1, "text": "Place a heavy skillet over medium heat and add the sugar. Cook, stirring occasionally with a fork, until the sugar is melted and a deep golden color. Remove from the heat and stir in the pecan halves. Pour the mixture onto a buttered sheet pan and allow to cool."}, {"position": 2, "text": "When cooled, chop the pralines finely. Set half aside and transfer the other half to the container of a food processor. Pulse until the mixture resembles coarse sand. Set aside."}, {"position": 3, "text": "Make or reheat the custard sauce (it should be warm) and transfer it to a large bowl. Dissolve the gelatin in 2 tablespoons water and stir it into the custard. Stir in the salt. Place the bowl in the refrigerator to cool, stirring occasionally, until it is thickened but not yet gelled, about 20 minutes."}, {"position": 4, "text": "Fold the powdered praline into the cooled custard. Whip 1 ¼ cups of the cream until it holds soft peaks and fold it into the custard. Fold all but ¼ cup of the reserved chopped praline pieces into the custard. Save the rest for a garnish. Refrigerate."}, {"position": 5, "text": "Take 4 to 6 ladyfingers and trim them into \"petals\" with one rounded edge and one narrow, pointed end. Arrange them, domed side down, in the bottom of an 8-cup charlotte mold, narrow ends pointing in and rounded ends pointed out. Trim the sides of another 12 to 15 ladyfingers and set them upright around the mold, domed sides out, packing them as closely together as possible."}, {"position": 6, "text": "Pour the custard mixture into the mold. Using a serrated knife, trim the tops of the ladyfingers level with the filling. Refrigerate for at least 4 hours."}, {"position": 7, "text": "Unmold the charlotte onto a serving platter. Combine the powdered sugar, remaining cup of cream and rum and whip until it holds firm peaks. Place some of the cream in a pastry bag fitted with a star tube and pipe lines of cream between the ladyfingers and rosettes around the edge of the plate. Sprinkle the charlotte with the reserved praline, slice and serve with the remaining sweetened whipped cream on the side."}], "nutrition": {"calories": 569.3, "protein_g": 6.8, "carbohydrates_g": 54.2, "fat_g": 37.6, "saturated_fat_g": 17.1, "trans_fat_g": 0.8, "cholesterol_mg": 131.9, "sodium_mg": 93.9, "fiber_g": 1.7, "sugar_g": 37.4, "unsaturated_fat_g": 18.1, "source": "nyt", "basis": "per_serving", "computed_with": null}, "tags": {"cuisine": [], "diet": [], "course": ["dessert"], "meal": [], "occasion": ["christmas"], "other": ["boil", "ladyfinger", "pecan"]}, "images": [], "rating": null, "video_url": null, "comment_count": null, "published_at": "1998-12-13", "scraped_at": "2026-09-07T01:52:19.059984+00:00", "scraper_version": "1.0.0"}
{"slug": "preserved-lemons-and-limes", "nyt_id": 10883, "url": "https://cooking.nytimes.com/recipes/10883-preserved-lemons-and-limes", "name": "Preserved Lemons And Limes", "author": "Molly O’Neill", "description": "This variation on a Moroccan compote adds a fresh note to chicken, pork or oily fish like bass, bluefish, tuna and swordfish, when rubbed in about an hour before grilling. To avoid a bitter taste, wipe excess off before grilling. Spoon fresh preserved lemon and lime over the grilled food before serving. It also makes a fine compliment to olives in a couscous, pasta or rice salad.", "servings": {"text": "About two cups", "min": 2, "max": 2, "yield_type": "volume"}, "time": {"prep_minutes": null, "cook_minutes": null, "total_minutes": 10, "raw": {"total": "10 minutes, plus 1 week's standing"}}, "ingredients": [{"position": 1, "raw": "2 lemons, each cut lengthwise into 8 wedges, seeds removed", "group": null, "quantity": 2.0, "quantity_max": null, "unit": null, "item": "lemons", "preparation": "each cut lengthwise into 8 wedges, seeds removed", "optional": false}, {"position": 2, "raw": "6 limes, each cut lengthwise into 6 wedges, seeds removed", "group": null, "quantity": 6.0, "quantity_max": null, "unit": null, "item": "limes", "preparation": "each cut lengthwise into 6 wedges, seeds removed", "optional": false}, {"position": 3, "raw": "½ cup kosher salt", "group": null, "quantity": 0.5, "quantity_max": null, "unit": "cup", "item": "kosher salt", "preparation": null, "optional": false}, {"position": 4, "raw": "⅔ cup fresh lemon juice", "group": null, "quantity": 0.6666666666666666, "quantity_max": null, "unit": "cup", "item": "fresh lemon juice", "preparation": null, "optional": false}], "steps": [{"position": 1, "text": "Bring a medium pot of water to a boil. Reduce to a simmer and drop in the lemons and limes. Simmer for 3 to 4 minutes. Drain. Combine the salt and lemon juice and pour a little in the bottom of a glass jar. Add the lemons and limes to the jar and pour in the remaining salt mixture. Seal tightly. Let stand for seven days, shaking the jar each day to redistribute the salt mixture. Rinse the salt off the lemons and limes before using."}], "nutrition": {"calories": 102.6, "protein_g": 2.6, "carbohydrates_g": 34.5, "fat_g": 0.8, "saturated_fat_g": 0.1, "trans_fat_g": 0.0, "cholesterol_mg": 0.0, "sodium_mg": 851.9, "fiber_g": 8.2, "sugar_g": 7.5, "unsaturated_fat_g": 0.3, "source": "nyt", "basis": "per_serving", "computed_with": null}, "tags": {"cuisine": ["middle eastern"], "diet": [], "course": [], "meal": [], "occasion": [], "other": ["boil", "condiments", "dips and spreads", "lemon", "lime", "project", "salt"]}, "images": [], "rating": {"average": 3.0, "count": 21}, "video_url": null, "comment_count": null, "published_at": "1993-08-01", "scraped_at": "2026-09-07T01:54:10.033819+00:00", "scraper_version": "1.0.0"}
{"slug": "pressure-cooker-squash-with-honey-and-lemongrass", "nyt_id": 12283, "url": "https://cooking.nytimes.com/recipes/12283-pressure-cooker-squash-with-honey-and-lemongrass", "name": "Pressure Cooker Squash With Honey and Lemongrass", "author": "Melissa Clark", "description": "This caramelized delicata squash purée, adapted from Nathan Myhrvold's multivolume “Modernist Cuisine: The Art and Science of Cooking,” is prepared in a pressure cooker. Normally, a pressure cooker wouldn’t get hot enough to caramelize anything. But, Mr. Myhrvold explained, if you create an alkaline environment with a sprinkle of baking soda, you can caramelize at a lower temperature. And the pressurized environment helps ingredients caramelize through and through, not just around the outside. This gives the squash an intense, nutty flavor, which is enhanced here with buckwheat honey and lemongrass. Melissa Clark", "servings": {"text": "10 servings", "min": 10, "max": 10, "yield_type": "servings"}, "time": {"prep_minutes": null, "cook_minutes": null, "total_minutes": 35, "raw": {"total": "35 minutes"}}, "ingredients": [{"position": 1, "raw": "4 tablespoons unsalted butter", "group": null, "quantity": 4.0, "quantity_max": null, "unit": "tbsp", "item": "unsalted butter", "preparation": null, "optional": false}, {"position": 2, "raw": "1 ½ pounds delicata squash, peeled, seeded and cut into ½-inch cubes (4 cups)", "group": null, "quantity": 1.5, "quantity_max": null, "unit": "lb", "item": "delicata squash", "preparation": "peeled, seeded and cut into ½-inch cubes (4 cups)", "optional": false}, {"position": 3, "raw": "½ lemongrass stalk", "group": null, "quantity": 0.5, "quantity_max": null, "unit": null, "item": "lemongrass stalk", "preparation": null, "optional": false}, {"position": 4, "raw": "1 teaspoon kosher salt", "group": null, "quantity": 1.0, "quantity_max": null, "unit": "tsp", "item": "kosher salt", "preparation": null, "optional": false}, {"position": 5, "raw": "½ teaspoon baking soda", "group": null, "quantity": 0.5, "quantity_max": null, "unit": "tsp", "item": "baking soda", "preparation": null, "optional": false}, {"position": 6, "raw": "Buckwheat honey or other honey", "group": null, "quantity": null, "quantity_max": null, "unit": null, "item": "buckwheat honey or other honey", "preparation": null, "optional": false}], "steps": [{"position": 1, "text": "Melt butter in a pressure cooker. Stir in squash, lemongrass, salt and baking soda. Cover tightly with pressure cooker lid and cook at a gauge pressure of 1 bar (15 p.s.i.) for 20 minutes. Begin the timing after the pressure has been reached."}, {"position": 2, "text": "Depressurize the cooker according to the manufacturer’s instructions."}, {"position": 3, "text": "Remove lemongrass. Blend the squash to a smooth purée. Season with honey to taste. Serve, or refrigerate and then reheat gently before serving."}], "nutrition": null, "tags": {"cuisine": [], "diet": ["gluten-free", "vegetarian"], "course": ["side dish"], "meal": [], "occasion": [], "other": ["five ingredient", "squash", "weeknight"]}, "images": [{"url": "https://static01.nyt.com/images/2012/01/18/dining/video-myhrvoldsquash/video-myhrvoldsquash-superJumbo.jpg", "alt": "", "primary": true}], "rating": {"average": 4.0, "count": 174}, "video_url": "https://www.nytimes.com/video/dining/100000001265540/in-the-kitchen-with-nathan-myhrvold.html", "comment_count": null, "published_at": "2012-01-17", "scraped_at": "2026-09-06T23:55:00.288374+00:00", "scraper_version": "1.0.0"}
